WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents episode 67

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.220
on June 2nd, 2026.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:06.780
Let's see what's shipped.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.900
Their signal and noise in AI every day.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.260
Let's get to the signal.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.500
Enterprise teams can now run governed AI agents

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.680
inside existing procurement systems instead of leaking data

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.620
to personal chat GPT accounts.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:22.080
ZIP launched five super agents

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.340
plus a native MCP implementation

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.580
that gives every action inside compliance controls.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.100
Alibaba released Quinn 3.7 plus

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.280
with vision, tool use, and autonomous iteration.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.920
JetBrains shipped Mellum 2,

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.400
a 12 billion parameter mixture of experts model

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.720
aimed at specialized coding workflows.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.500
Nvidia expanded its agent tooling

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.120
for both PCs and enterprise deployments.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.540
ZIP announced five super agents

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.200
and a procurement native model context protocol implementation.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.960
The implementation connects its platform

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.140
directly to Clawed, chat GPT,

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.540
and other MCP compatible assistance

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.940
while preserving roles, permissions, and full audit trails.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:09.260
The agents run on a shared land graph-based execution engine

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.700
with separate pre-processing orchestration synthesis

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.180
and post-processing nodes.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.480
The orchestration node uses a react loop

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.660
to decide between vector search, structured API calls,

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.000
or policy lookups.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.700
Every high-impact action still routes

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:25.920
through human checkpoints.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.680
The system explicitly avoids training on customer data,

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.640
via zero retention agreements with model providers.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.000
Procurement teams at OpenAI and Thropic,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.160
Block, and Snowflake are already using the platform.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.920
And Thropic doubled its procurement volume

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.140
without adding headcount.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.120
The super agents and MCP server are in beta now

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.640
with general availability expected this summer.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.600
This development continues the maturation of agent tool use

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.380
and the MCP interoperability layer

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:57.760
of the show has been tracking.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.400
Attentive listeners should watch for how other platforms

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.020
adopt similar governed execution patterns.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:09.280
Alibaba's twin team released Quen 3.7 plus

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.020
on the Bayley-N platform.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.300
The model adds vision and video understanding,

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.600
deep reasoning, tool invocation,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.700
and autonomous iteration capabilities.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.580
It is positioned for self-programming

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.280
and multi-step agent workflows,

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:24.760
rather than pure chat.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.620
No parameter count or benchmark numbers

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.200
were disclosed in the announcement.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.160
Builders working on vision grounded agents

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.720
should test the Bayley-N integration this week.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.300
JetBrains released Mellum 2,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.680
a 12 billion parameter mixture of experts model.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.940
It was trained on 10.6 trillion tokens

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.520
and licensed under Apache 2.0.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.040
The model targets AI-assisted coding workflows

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.680
where low latency matters more than frontier reasoning depth.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.620
No MMLU or coding specific benchmarks were shared.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.800
Developers already inside JetBrains tools

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.540
should try swapping it into existing agent chains

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.720
for narrow code editing sub-tasks.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.460
This open-weight release fits the ongoing narrowing

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:12.080
of the performance gap with closed frontier models.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.360
Open AI frontier models and codecs

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.100
are now generally available on Amazon bedrock.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.540
Enterprises can use existing security compliance

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.480
and governance workflows without new integrations.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.520
The move also signals future availability

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.660
of cybersecurity capabilities such as daybreak.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.120
No pricing or specific model versions were listed.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.040
Teams already on bedrock should evaluate

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:39.760
whether routing open AI calls through the service

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.080
reduces their current compliance overhead.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.220
A developer around a multi-agent orchestrator

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:49.380
on Quinn 3.6 to 27b via Alama

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.000
on a single 3,090 graphics card for two weeks.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.160
Plan generation stayed close to cloud quality

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.940
while tool call format errors hit roughly 12%.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.600
Long context drift appeared past 14,000 tokens.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.400
The test showed the model works as a planner

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.300
when every tool call is gated,

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.900
but not as an autonomous executor.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.200
Nvidia released new tools aimed at enterprise AI agents.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.660
The announcement builds on its existing

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.760
agentic AI PC and data center platforms.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.580
It emphasizes support across literally every computer maker

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:27.120
for the RTX Spark agentic platform.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:30.620
No specific SDK names, install commands

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.620
or benchmark numbers were provided.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.080
Teams evaluating on device agents should watch

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.160
for the first public RTX Spark SDK release.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.200
Jensen Huang positioned RTX Spark

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.380
as an attempt to reinvent the single most important tool

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:46.020
of humanity.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:49.160
The platform targets local agent execution

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.140
rather than cloud only workflows.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:53.620
No concrete performance numbers

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.240
or early access program details were released.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.420
Developers building local agents should monitor

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.160
partner PC announcements for the first devices

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.220
shipping with the stack.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.340
Simon Willison reported that the codex desktop app

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.840
has been updated and restored.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.080
The update returns local codex functionality

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.040
after a period of unavailability.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.460
No version number or change log details were shared.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.160
Users who rely on the desktop client should reinstall

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.240
and test current compatibility with their local workflows.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.840
GitHub switched co-pilot to token based billing.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.660
Early users are reporting price increases

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.820
compared with the previous flat monthly rate.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:36.420
The change took effect the day after the April announcement

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.360
window closed.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:41.180
No exact per token rates or organization level

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.520
examples were published.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.460
Teams should audit their current usage patterns

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.120
before the next billing cycle.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.780
Hugging faces Niels Rage added conference support

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:52.520
to paperswithcode.co.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:56.980
The site now indexes all CVPR 2026 papers

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:00.700
with RGUYVIDs, task categories, GitHub links,

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.280
and Hugging face artifacts.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.320
Users can filter accepted oral and spotlight papers.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:09.240
Researchers preparing for next week's conference

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.460
can use the new view to quickly surface relevant agent

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:13.860
and multimodal work.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.860
A new benchmark tested five frontier models

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:20.380
on 20 real CVEs across 18 Python projects.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.660
Agents were scored on whether they produced a correct

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:26.820
security patch that also passed hidden regression tests.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.940
Best solve rate reached 50% overall

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.720
and 60% under full advisory prompts.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:36.080
No model reliably identified vulnerabilities

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.340
from file and function hints alone.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.100
The work highlights that passing tests

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.580
does not guarantee the vulnerabilities closed.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.680
Okay, let's pop the hood on non-Kudah inference realities.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:50.460
Everyone talks about running local agents

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.020
as if Kudah is the only practical path.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.640
In practice, cycle and a rock M back ends

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.340
forced different memory layouts and kernel choices

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.460
that change both throughput and developer effort.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:04.560
On Intel Arc hardware,

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:11.180
Quinn 3.6 to 35ba3b reached 977 tokens

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.180
per second prompt processing at Q4K

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.600
with a 262,000 context window.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:21.880
That result came only after careful NGL and thread tuning.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.940
The same workload on ROCM still requires manual work

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:29.320
around for MI50 cards and lacks mature flash

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:30.560
attention equivalents.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:33.520
The quality gap is smaller than the tooling gap.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.320
Models themselves run yet missing optimized kernels

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.600
for common agent operations like structured JSON decoding

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:44.960
or long context summarization keep non-Kudah stacks behind.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:48.040
When the workload is a small number of parallel

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:51.340
30 billion class agents and you already own the hardware,

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.660
the non-Kudah route is worth the extra setup time.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.800
Otherwise, the Kudah ecosystem still wins

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.760
on both speed of iteration and library availability.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:03.220
The Gacha that bites most teams

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:06.500
is assuming a working Lama.cpp binary

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:08.960
means production ready agent throughput.

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:13.680
Test Zip's MCP server inside cloud or chat GPT

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.520
if your team already uses procurement tools.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:20.180
It surfaces governed data without leaving audit trails.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:24.420
Try Quen 3.7 plus on Baylian.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:27.280
For any vision plus tool use agent loop

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:30.020
you have been running on a cloud 3.5.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.380
Swap mellum 2 into a narrow code editing sub agent

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.680
inside an existing JetBrains workflow

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:39.240
to measure latency versus larger models.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.040
Run the CVE bench tasks against your current agent setup

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.440
to see where it fails the hidden regression tests

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:48.560
even when surface tests pass.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.400
Check the new papers with code.co-cvpr view

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.660
for agent-related papers before the conference next week.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:59.700
Zip's super agents and MCP move

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.160
from beta to general availability this summer.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:06.020
First RTX Spark enabled PCs are expected

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:08.620
from multiple hardware partners in the coming months.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.660
CVPR 2026 begins next week in Denver

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.860
with new agent and multimodal tracks.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:19.260
Further open AI capability expansions on AWS bedrock

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:21.720
including daybreak cybersecurity features

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.340
are planned but undated.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.860
Tomorrow keep an eye on the first public RTX Spark SDK release

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.100
and any early partner PC announcements.

